{"name":"Pumice Stone","hex":"#cac2b9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pumice stone","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A piece of pumice.","Pumice (the substance)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pumice_stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pumice","blocks":[{"text":"Pumice, called pumicite in its powdered or dust form, is a volcanic rock that consists of extremely vesicular rough-textured volcanic glass, which may or may not contain crystals. It is typically light-colored. Scoria is another vesicular volcanic rock that differs from pumice in having larger vesicles, thicker vesicle walls, and being dark-colored and denser."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7b/Teidepumice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pumice","wikidata":"Q180044"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)","wikidata":"Q8063"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Kuthiny_Baty.jpg/500px-Kuthiny_Baty.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Kuthiny_Baty.jpg/500px-Kuthiny_Baty.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kutkhiny Baty, a pumice rock formation outcrop located 4 km from the source of the Ozyornaya River (Lake Kurile), near the southern tip of the Kamchatka Peninsula, Russia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKuthiny_Baty.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Pumice_from_Sinai_in_Egypt_-_Thin_Section.png/500px-Pumice_from_Sinai_in_Egypt_-_Thin_Section.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Pumice_from_Sinai_in_Egypt_-_Thin_Section.png/500px-Pumice_from_Sinai_in_Egypt_-_Thin_Section.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Thin section of pumice from Sinai Peninsula, Egypt","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APumice_from_Sinai_in_Egypt_-_Thin_Section.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Pumice_stone_detail444.jpg/250px-Pumice_stone_detail444.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Pumice_stone_detail444.jpg/500px-Pumice_stone_detail444.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Pumice_stone_detail444.jpg/250px-Pumice_stone_detail444.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APumice_stone_detail444.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Akrotiri_Santorini-Volcano_pumice-layers_Aegean-Sea.jpg/120px-Akrotiri_Santorini-Volcano_pumice-layers_Aegean-Sea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_